{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact R3 revision 11 head 332dd485059cddb4c946f9c53c054ddbe91a7dcc, direct parent 49e6321eaae56c3f23b884c9bd2a0cd5194bdbdc. This is ordinary independent three-subject design review under 1026ed6a/567606e3, not source acceptance or adoption.\n\nP2 — Correct the current R1 authority binding. Main lines 137 and 142–166 state that R1 revision 7 is in force, revision 10 at f52573e7 is unadopted, and an unqualified R1 section means revision 7. The same normative restriction holds the license decision declaration at 1784–1785 and capacity accounting at 2623–2625 pending adoption; the implementation gate at 4619, status matrix at 4764–4772, sibling agreement at 4856–4858 and closing claim at 4929 repeat it. Corrections lines 513–519 and later current-status statements repeat the restriction. R1 revision 10 was already adopted by effective planner report 85c18b66, following approval 98b470c3 and delivery 1a2b9102; c24fdfa0 ratifies that delivery. The main artifact's statement itself claims reliance on that adopted contract. Following the current prose would send an implementer to the obsolete contract and withhold already adopted preparation, retained-input and settlement accounting. Update all present-tense authority rules, current matrices and implementation gates to the adopted exact contract and its adoption event. Preserve genuinely dated read history. Keep R3 revision 11's own declarations and G1–G18 proposals or gaps distinct: changing R1's authority must not adopt this candidate, invent the G15 refused-entry marker form, or claim later I1 reconciliation complete.\n\nThe two P2 findings in 74b21dc8 are repaired structurally in this candidate. The first decision of a numbered license request bound to the standing receiver pin consumes that request's own reservation, applied or refused, records the highest decided number, and prevents a duplicate or an unrelated request from consuming it (1773–1848). Request 1 remains new work; the G15 executable marker form remains expressly owed. The first authentic answer on an unsuperseded timed-out job replaces the deadline as its deciding entry, while passed, failed, errored and superseded jobs keep later answers as history (3853–3903). The late-pass witness and destination's check now agree. The destination witness includes its one closing checkpoint and conserves the 500-entry budget at every row. The added withdrawal declaration is limited to one reserved decision for the correct operation and sender; other written refusals remain new work.\n\nNonblocking correction — State the member-byte unit deliberately. Main 300–309 proposes 256 canonical bytes and calls that I1's existing temporary value. At exact I1 97518f68, packages/derive/src/values.ts:22 counts raw UTF-8 handle bytes; validate.ts:922 separately reserves up to six bytes per raw byte for canonical escaping. Even a 256-byte ASCII handle occupies 258 bytes as a quoted canonical string. The canonical policy can be an explicitly different, stricter proposal; alternatively use the existing raw UTF-8 unit. Correct the attribution and reconcile that choice with R1/I1 before implementation. R4 still owns the number and G12 owns the character grammar. This is not an overflow finding or a new migration requirement.\n\nArchitecture: The full platform, authority, publication, workspace, device, provider, replay, retirement and interruption scope remains present. Both repairs reuse existing deciding entries and request reservations. Closure, checkpoint rearming, unknown attempts and distinct duties remain explicit. G15–G18 correctly retain marker forms, creator definition sourcing, retransmission liveness and I1 choices after 114 as owned work. The historical 139-choice count is qualified; the current 152-choice reconciliation remains under R1 successor 108ea663 and existing R3/R4 owners. No historical read or candidate declaration supplies implementation closure.\n\nSecurity: Credential replacement, session expiry, revocation and final publication do not silently settle unknown writers or cleanup. Member-authored text grants no authority; the injection boundary witness remains owed. H1–H6 are operational assumptions, not independently established facts. No additional high-confidence material security defect found in these three subjects.\n\nSimplification: Preserve the shared first-decision and reservation rules. Correct the current authority references consistently rather than add another approval gate, timer or settlement mechanism. Complete existing owned gaps without reducing scope.\n\nCoverage: Two independent delegated reviewers freshly read every byte of all three whole current subjects and every added and removed byte across the two changed paths. Main: 437023 bytes/4932 LF, SHA-256 8c6ba1af6f3316052a9d22da2f35f109f11c4ecc1bf8c06e310787826e4a22ae. Corrections: 88997 bytes/645 LF, SHA-256 4dcd79c095f4c577b85dd18fd3e736849984a5f4a8ddabefeba263636fd4c166. Facts: 84111 bytes/723 LF, SHA-256 12db56d1ba4a9681e47408ebfc7cbfa75495163592a3e54bed4b85f71e76f8e0; unchanged from the parent and freshly read as historical evidence. Changes: 754 added and 167 removed lines. Root independently read the current opening authority rules, complete license declaration, capacity closure/refusal/checkpoint clauses and witness, late-job rule and witness, current authority matrices and exact I1 member-byte source. All truncated reading output was recovered; root does not claim a personal whole-document read.\n\nStatic source, document and provenance review only. No project execution, private diagnostics, provider effects, independent runtime or whole-product cost proof. This verdict requires a corrected review candidate under the existing request and closes no implementation or originating delivery duty.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dd9ca6a8cf77be3ffd36d08f088c20f1a751736e","binding":"evidence-only","head":"332dd485059cddb4c